<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">British Central Africa Protectorate</h1><p class="st-meta">1891 CE – 1907 CE · capital Zomba</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British protectorate around Lake Malawi, established through wars against slave-trading chiefs and later renamed Nyasaland.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Scottish missions at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blantyre">Blantyre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livingstonia">Livingstonia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-lakes-company">African Lakes Company</a> drew <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a> region. To block Portuguese claims, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> declared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shire-highlands">Shire Highlands</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1889 and in 1891 proclaimed the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> Protectorate, under Commissioner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-johnston">Harry Johnston</a> with his headquarters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zomba">Zomba</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-johnston">Johnston</a> and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-sharpe">Alfred Sharpe</a> used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> and African troops to defeat Yao slave-trading chiefs such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/makanjira-chiefdom">Makanjira</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jumbe-state-of-nkhotakota">Jumbe State of Nkhotakota</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/maseko-ngoni-kingdom">Maseko Ngoni Kingdom</a>. European planters grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shire-highlands">Shire Highlands</a>, relying on labour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenancy">tenancy</a> known as thangata, and a hut tax pushed men into wage work.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1907 the territory was renamed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nyasaland">Nyasaland Protectorate</a>, with a governor and legislative council. Its borders became those of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>, and its planter economy and migrant labour persisted into the colonial period.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/british-central-africa-protectorate.jpg" alt="British Central Africa Protectorate" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Fenn-O-maniC · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_British_Central_Africa_Protectorate.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1891 CE – 1907 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zomba</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Harry Johnston, Alfred Sharpe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Islam, traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chichewa, Chiyao, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malawi">Malawi</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Central Africa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1899&amp;focus=british-central-africa-protectorate" aria-label="British Central Africa Protectorate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5644 2750 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where British Central Africa Protectorate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5964 3006L5932 2859M5964 3006L5941 2915M5964 3006L4997 651M5964 3006L5964 3006"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5932 2859h0M5941 2915h0M4997 651h0M5964 3006h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5964 3006h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5964 3006h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zomba. <a href="/atlas?year=1899&amp;focus=british-central-africa-protectorate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
